WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.630
One of the interesting aspects
of the story of Christ and the

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:07.440
Christian story in general, is
that within the story of Christ,

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.250
you see that Christ chose his
own betrayer. And this, of

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.870
course, is a testimony to how in
a larger sense, in Christianity,

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.290
we have the sense that
Antichrist will rise out of

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:24.540
Christianity, will appear as a
side effect or a kind of strange

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.630
deformation of the Christian
message. And so there are

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.200
several aspects to Antichrist.
I'm going to look especially at

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.130
one, which I called something
like weaponized compassion. And

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:39.270
we will see this version or this
aspect of antichrist, especially

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:43.200
in the story of Judas Iscariot,
who is the disciple that Christ

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.870
chose, who ultimately ended up
betraying him.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:14.820
And so there are several aspects
of antichrist. There are two

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.660
aspects which we could see
almost as something like opposed

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.680
to each other or two extremes of
what Antichrist looks like. The

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.920
one that people usually tend to
look upon is the Empire idea of

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.180
Antichrist or the beast, this
notion of Rome as being

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.880
something like the horror of
Babylon, all this kind of

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.400
imagery of Antichrist. And it's
especially happened in the

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.280
Protestant Reformation where,
you know, the notion of the

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.890
relationship between the
Catholic Church and Rome was

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.780
related to a certain extent with
the idea of a parody of

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.200
Christ and of Christ's message
and something like Antichrist.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.330
And there's something about
that which is interesting,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.530
because there is an aspect in
the story of Antichrist

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:06.090
which is related to
St. Peter, because St. Peter is,

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.690
of course, the one who denies
Christ. St. Peter is also the

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:13.710
one who, although he is the
first one to recognise Christ,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:19.620
he ultimately refuses one aspect
of Christ, refuses for a certain

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.440
amount of time, one aspect of
Christ's message, which is that

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.120
Christ must die. And when St.
Peter wants to oppose that,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.590
wants to say that Christ will
not die on the cross, then that

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:37.050
is when Christ tells St. Peter,
"Get behind the Satan." And so,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.690
of course, St. Peter is
important and there's a very

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.290
positive aspect of St. Peter,
which is true, and which is good

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:43.900
to understand.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.200
But the story of Christ is very
complex, and contains all this

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.860
subtlety within it. And one of
the aspects of St. Peter, of the

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.000
strength of the Christian story
and the strength of the, let's

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.600
say, the possibilities of
Christianity, also has a dark

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:03.610
side, which is the possibility
of pride, the possibility of not

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.730
thinking that Christianity is
about self sacrifice, and the

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.300
willingness to die for others
and the willingness to die in

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.480
general. But there is also a
flip side to that image of

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:18.910
Antichrist, which is that
although Christ tells us to care

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.480
for the poor, tells us to tend
to the orphan and the widow and

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.360
to care for the weak, to be
attentive to the margins,

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.693
be attentive to those that are
usually excluded, cast out,

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.550
there's also a dark side to
that, which is the Antichrist

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:36.970
aspect of it, which is something
that we could call weaponized

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:41.560
compassion. And this version of
weaponized compassion appears in

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:45.280
the other disciple in the story
of Christ that has to do with

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.330
this Antichrist aspect and that
is, of course, Judas Iscariot.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.630
Judas Iscariot is the one who
betrays Christ ultimately,

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.900
betrays him with a kiss. And
this is related to this

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.200
weaponized compassion that we
see in the story of Judas. And

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.380
that we will especially see in
the story where Mary of Bethany

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.530
washes Christ's feet. So I'm
going to read the text with you.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.860
And we're going to look at
what's going on in the story.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:13.390
And what that can mean for us,
especially today, because we are

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:18.010
faced with some of that. And it
tends to confuse Christians,

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:22.360
just like also when Christianity
is strong and Empire-like it can

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.840
also let's say, confuse some
Christians as to what the real

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.023
goal of Christianity is.

00:04:28.023 --> 00:04:31.920
And so the texts appeared in the
Gospel of St. John, chapter 12,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.850
starting at verse 1. 'Six days
before the Passover, Jesus

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.540
therefore came to Bethany, where
Lazarus was whom Jesus had

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.620
raised from the dead, so they
gave a dinner for him. Martha

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.620
served and Lazarus was one of
those reclining with him at

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.790
table. Mary therefore took a
pound of expensive ointment,

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.840
made from pure nard and anointed
the feet of Jesus and wiped His

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.710
feet with her hair. The house
was filled with the fragrance of

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:02.280
the perfume but Judas is
Iscariot, one of his disciples,

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:06.720
who was about to betray him,
said, "Why was this ointment not

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.060
sold for 300 denari and given to
the poor?" He said this, not

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.020
because he cared for the poor,
but because he was a thief. And

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:20.130
having charge of the money bag,
he used to help himself to what

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:25.080
was put into it. Jesus said,
"Leave her alone, so that she

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:31.140
may keep it for the day of my
burial, for the poor you always

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.860
have with you, but you do not
always have me."'

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:41.530
And so what's going on in this
story? We have an image of a

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:47.020
woman who takes very expensive,
takes something very expensive,

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:52.660
and focuses it on worship. Now
we could understand that as many

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.720
things, of course, we can
understand it in general of the

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.140
idea of creating beauty in order
to worship God, we could also

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:02.560
understand it in terms of the
amount of attention, the amount

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:08.650
of time and resources and
valuable things that we put into

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.881
the service of worshipping
Christ.

00:06:11.881 --> 00:06:15.550
And so the trick that Judas
wants to take is he wants to

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.120
take a good, which is caring for
the poor. Now, of course, caring

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.360
for the poor is a good, not only
is it a good, but it is a good

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:27.310
that Christ Himself has asked us
to do. Christ is constantly

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:31.270
telling people to care for the
poor, and to help the weak. But

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:35.470
he's trying to make that good
into the highest good. And this

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:39.970
is where there is a problem. And
so he tells the woman that she

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.320
should not have used this
expensive thing, this precious

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.090
thing, to worship Christ, but
that she should have used it and

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:55.360
sold it and given it to the
poor. Now, what's interesting in

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.250
this is, this is why I called it
weaponized compassion. Because

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.260
ultimately, Judas doesn't care
for the poor. What Judas wants

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:10.120
is to acquire power for himself.
And so he wants her to sell this

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.990
for money, so that he can dip
into the money bag and take the

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:17.230
money for himself. Now, of
course, this is very specific in

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:20.410
the story. But you can
understand that in many ways,

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:26.230
which is that, many people can
use compassion, or use

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:31.360
compassion as a weapon in order
to acquire power for themselves.

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:36.100
Now, this is a temptation which
is becoming stronger and

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.910
stronger in the Christian
church. It is something which is

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:43.870
blinding many Christians to some
aspects of what is going on

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:47.410
around them. And because they
don't know how to react to it.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.760
There is a tendency to make
people feel guilty about their

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:55.780
own Christian values, to make
them feel as if those very

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:58.870
Christian values, the very
Christian beliefs they have, is

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:04.120
somehow a compromise in
compassion. And that if they

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:08.350
were truly compassionate, they
would actually sacrifice their

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:09.820
own Christian beliefs.

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.740
The ultimate version of
something like this is in the

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:18.060
movie 'Silence', which many of
you have probably seen. In this

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:23.580
movie a Christian missionary is
sent to Japan. And the Christian

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.700
missionary is faced with a
serious problem, which is that

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:31.800
in trying to support the
Christian church there, the

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:36.210
Japanese authorities realise
that if we torture the

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:39.360
missionaries, we have a problem
because the missionaries are

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.830
willing to die for their faith.
But if we torture other people,

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.630
then that is putting the right
pressure on the missionaries,

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:53.670
because their own values, their
own compassion, will compel them

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:58.770
to deny their faith in order to
preserve the life of others. And

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:03.000
so, of course, I can't imagine
being in that situation. It's a

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.660
horrible situation to be in. And
I don't want to judge anybody

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:10.080
who's in this situation who
would have compromised or

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.380
fallen. But the very fact that
this is framed in the story,

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:17.400
that in the story there is a
sense in which although the

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:21.900
missionary denied his faith, and
ended up completely denying his

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:26.280
own belief in Christ and his own
attachment to God, ultimately

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:30.840
there was something holy about
that very action. And it's shown

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:36.150
that in the end, when he's
dying, and he's being cremated,

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:38.940
we see that there's a little
cross hidden on him or something

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:44.670
like that. And that is to show
that somehow the very denial of

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:48.840
his faith was done out of
compassion. And that this would

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.890
be the ultimate Christian act,
in a way, would be to deny your

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.690
faith out of compassion.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:59.760
And so you can apply this
problem to many, many other

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.880
things. You can apply it, of
course, right now to many social

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.600
issues in which those social
issues are related to things

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.020
that Christians hold dear in
their values. But there is a

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:14.970
pressure, there's a pressure on
Christians, to, out of

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:19.530
compassion, to deny their own
stance. That is, not only have

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:23.220
compassion for the weak, have
compassion for the sinners, have

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:26.970
compassion for people who are
not able to hold the standard,

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:31.290
because who can? No one, no
Christian can hold the ultimate

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:36.000
standard that Christ lays out
for us. But that compromising on

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.440
your standard would be a more
Christian thing to do, than to

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:44.400
simply love others and be
compassionate while holding to

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.490
the highest standard. Now
there's a place where you see

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:51.240
that happen all the time. But
there's another aspect of this

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:54.990
which is going on right now,
which is related almost directly

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:01.140
to the story of Judas and the
notion of compromising worship

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.770
in the name of something else,
compromising worship, in the

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:05.970
name of compassion.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:09.830
There is a narrative which is
being pushed, and which is very

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:15.260
strong. And it's strong, because
it's so long, it's enduring. You

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:19.160
know, it's been two years now,
where people are being told that

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:22.460
out of compassion - that
Christians shouldn't commune and

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:26.540
worship together, out of
compassion for others. That is,

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:30.530
that out of a desire to preserve
the weak and to preserve the

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:35.270
helpless people
should sacrifice worship, and

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:39.650
should not practice the
Christian practices of singing

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:44.300
together before their God, of
communing together to the Holy

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:50.300
Sacraments. And this is a very,
very fascinating thing, because

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:56.630
you can see that in a way it is,
it is a very strong tool. It is

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:01.310
a very strong tool because it
dives right into what Christians

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:05.570
believe. And it dives right into
the Christian's high values and

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:11.180
tries to set those values up as
the highest above what is more

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:17.150
important, which is worship and
submission to God. And you see

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:21.350
it, you actually see it very
interestingly, you can notice

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:25.520
that if you look at the
difference between Pope Benedict

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:28.100
and Pope Francis, it's very
fascinating, because Pope

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:32.390
Benedict wanted to bring back
worship. And so he brought out

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:35.090
some old crosses that were in
treasuries and wanted to kind of

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:38.600
show the beauty with vestments,
and this beautiful

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.630
aspect of the Christian
liturgical services and

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:46.100
Christian worship. And he was
eviscerated, he was eviscerated

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:49.820
by the media, eviscerated by
people, ultimately, who hate

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:53.900
Christianity, let's be honest.
But nonetheless, there was a

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:58.250
manner in which people gobbled
it up, and people completely

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:02.990
gave in to this narrative. And
now here comes Pope Francis, who

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:08.420
is the very opposite, who tends
to, let's say, play down the

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:12.200
worship aspect of the church and
play up the social aspect of the

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:15.650
church, play up the idea of
helping the poor, and there's

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:18.050
absolutely nothing wrong - Of
course, it is wonderful for

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:21.650
Christians to help the poor, but
the notion that we should, the

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:25.910
notion that worshipping Christ
is somehow bad, and that putting

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:31.190
valuable things in the service
and worship of God is somehow

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:37.430
horrible and disconnected.
Whereas helping the poor is

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:39.500
something that everybody loves,
and everybody finds is

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:44.330
beautiful, I think is definitely
an issue. And it's an issue that

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:49.520
although I sympathise with
Christians who struggle to see

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:53.060
the normal hierarchy, this is
actually the normal hierarchy of

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:56.240
being. This is really
representing the manner in which

00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:59.870
attention to the highest,
worship of God, you know,

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:05.780
placing yourself in the right
position towards God, alone and

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:09.590
together into communion. And,
you know, engaging in the

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:13.760
liturgical dance, engaging in
the grand singing and dancing of

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:20.870
worship is more important than
the social actions of the

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:24.680
church. And of course, I know
some people are gonna watch this

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.700
and going to get angry and think
that I am somehow diminishing or

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:32.330
bringing down the idea of
helping the poor and of course

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:36.440
not, I spent seven years of my
life as a volunteer in Africa in

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:40.010
the desire just to serve Christ
in that manner. But nonetheless,

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:46.340
I still need to re-emphasize the
natural, the normal hierarchy of

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:51.560
things, which is that if you try
to sacrifice the top part for

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:55.280
the second part, you are going
to break down and you can see

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:58.970
that in many of the churches
that have embraced social

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:02.360
justice, no manner how they
phrase it as their highest

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:08.810
value, higher than their creed,
higher than their own original

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:14.330
beliefs, higher than the values
which were given to them, you

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:17.870
know, from the apostles all the
way - Then, and they're willing

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:21.650
to even sometimes criticize
scripture, criticize

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:26.240
the saints, in order to
bring about this social justice

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:30.230
transformation that they want.
That's not going to work, it's

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:33.410
going to break down, it's going
to lead to a breakdown, because

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:38.120
it doesn't follow the normal -
If you focus on secondary goods

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:42.410
as the highest good no matter
how good it is, it will

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:44.750
ultimately lead to a breakdown.

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:48.680
One of the key ways to
recognise if what is being used

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:51.290
or what is being promoted is
something like weaponized

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:56.090
compassion is to look at the
power aspect. Because one of the

00:15:56.420 --> 00:16:00.350
ways that we can see it in the
story of Judas is that Judas is

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:05.210
telling someone else to be
compassionate, and is

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:09.890
reproaching someone else for not
being compassionate with the

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:14.660
purpose of gaining power for
themselves. And so this is the

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:19.880
best way to see whether or not
this is weaponized compassion.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:23.420
And I think that we have seen
plenty of that in the past two

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:28.520
years. We have seen plenty of
authorities tell us that we need

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:33.470
to act in this or that way, in
order to be compassionate. And

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:36.290
what's interesting is that, you
know, on the one hand, there's

00:16:36.290 --> 00:16:39.110
this idea that you are not being
compassionate, you are being

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:42.770
selfish by not doing this or
that. But then when you look

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:47.090
behind you the same authorities
telling you that are holding a

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:51.530
massive baseball bat over your
head. And the consequence for

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:56.000
not being compassionate is that
you will feel the brunt of their

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:02.030
raw power on you. And you'll
also notice that ultimately the

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:05.810
action that they want you to
pose is to the increase of their

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:09.410
power on you, and the increase
of their power on others. And so

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:13.940
this is the way in which you can
recognise weaponized compassion,

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:18.500
it's that, it is ultimately a
making you feel bad about

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:23.990
something, in order to acquire
power for the people who are

00:17:24.110 --> 00:17:25.160
promoting this.

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:29.050
Compassion is something which is
freely given. Compassion is

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:32.830
something which we should always
be examining ourselves to see

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:35.950
whether or not we are being
compassionate, and what is the

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:40.210
motivation of our actions. We
should not be trying to impose

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:43.810
compassion on others. This is
something which you do not see

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:48.460
in the Christian story, you
know. That is, there is no law

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:51.190
towards compassion, there is no
rule which makes you

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:53.860
compassionate. You can follow
all the rules you want,

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:57.220
compassion is a disposition of
the heart, compassion is a

00:17:57.220 --> 00:18:00.880
disposition of the person, it is
not about rules and about

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:04.330
following this or that thing.
You could act in ways that look

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:07.780
very compassionate on the
outside and are very selfish

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:12.220
inside, and vice versa. And so,
compassion is not something

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:16.210
which is subject to the law, it
is something which is subject to

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:19.180
the heart. And this is, of
course, the very difference that

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:24.010
Christ brings to us, which is
the difference between the

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:28.720
externals and the external law,
which is useful and helpful, and

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:34.600
the inner true reason of why
someone is acting. And so when

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:38.590
you see someone or when you see
an organisation try to impose

00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:44.170
compassion by law, or try to
make you feel as if by not being

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:48.310
compassionate you are going to
receive the full brunt of the

00:18:48.310 --> 00:18:51.760
modern state, then we have a
serious problem.

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:56.620
And so, of course, I do not want
to say that there isn't also the

00:18:56.620 --> 00:19:02.320
other aspect of Antichrist,
which is the aspect of believing

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:04.960
that Christianity is a political
weapon, believing that

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:07.960
Christianity is a tool for
nationalism. All these things

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:12.850
exist, and they exist now, let's
be honest, the prideful aspect

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:17.950
of the Antichrist. But the other
aspect, the weaponized

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:21.310
compassion one, is an aspect,
which I think is more

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:24.580
misunderstood today. And it
would be helpful for us to

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:28.420
understand that as we continue
to face the crisis that we are

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:31.990
dealing with, you know, that
we've been dealing with for the

00:19:31.990 --> 00:19:33.000
past few years.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.790
And so I hope this was helpful
to help you understand some

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:39.060
aspects of this pattern and I'll
talk to you very soon.

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:43.230
As you know, the Symbolic World
is not just a bunch of videos on

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:48.270
YouTube. We are also a podcast,
which you can find on your usual

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:52.020
podcast platform. But we also
have a website with a blog and

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:55.680
several very interesting
articles by very intelligent

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.740
people that have been thinking
about symbolism on all kinds of

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:02.160
subjects. We also have a clips
channel, a Facebook group. You

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:04.470
know, there's a whole lot of
ways that you can get more

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:07.920
involved in the exploration and
the discussion of symbolism.

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:11.070
Don't forget that my brother
Matthieu wrote a book, called

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:14.460
The Language of Creation, which
is a very powerful synthesis of

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:18.270
a lot of the ideas that I
explore. And so please go ahead

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:21.810
and explore this world. You can
also participate by, you know,

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:24.540
buying things that I've
designed, t-shirts with

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:27.540
different designs on them. And
you can also support this

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:30.510
podcast and these videos,
through Paypal or through

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:34.710
Patreon. Everybody who supports
